{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '25,196p' docs/design/dao.jing.implementation-plan.md | nl -ba -v25\"","aggregated_output":"    25\t## The invariants\n    26\t\n    27\tEach is marked with its source: **[D]** stated in `dao.jing.md`; **[T]** pinned\n    28\tby an existing test; **[T→D]** pinned by a test, not stated in the design, and\n    29\tjudged a real invariant that P3 adds to `dao.jing.md`; **[T✗]** pinned by a\n    30\ttest, judged an accident of the old implementation, and dropped. Every\n    31\t**[T✗]** is named so the implementer knows it was seen, not missed. Each group\n    32\tmaps to one test namespace. The E group additionally says which side of the\n    33\tobservation seam guarantees it (Decision 0).\n    34\t\n    35\t### A. Content addressing — `jing_test`\n    36\t\n    37\t- A1 [D] The address is derived from the payload alone:\n    38\t  `(segment-key x)` is `:segment/sha256-<64 lowercase hex>` and nothing else\n    39\t  enters it — not the source stream, not arrival order, not pool position.\n    40\t- A2 [D] Equal values address equally on every host. `content-hash` is\n    41\t  order-insensitive over maps and sets and total over every value, `nil`\n    42\t  included. Pinned by SHA-256 known-answer vectors on all three hosts\n    43\t  (`jing_test.cljc:56-77`), the only check that cljd's hand-rolled digest\n    44\t  mints the same addresses as the JVM and Node.\n    45\t- A3 [T→D] Distinct values address distinctly across types: `42` and `\"42\"`\n    46\t  do not collide. The design's \"equal supported values produce the same\n    47\t  bytes\" leaves the converse implicit; it belongs in *Canonical encoding*.\n    48\t- A4 [T→D] Minted addresses are readable EDN: the name never starts with a\n    49\t  digit, and a key survives `pr-str` → `read-string`. Addresses cross every\n    50\t  EDN boundary in the system; the `sha256-` prefix is load-bearing for that.\n    51\t- A5 [D] `segment-address?` is the strict test, and `segment-hash` throws on\n    52\t  anything that is not a valid address — foreign namespace, wrong length,\n    53\t  non-hex, uppercase.\n    54\t- A6 [D] The encoder is transitional (order-normalized `pr-str`), and\n    55\t  `content-hash`, `segment-key` and every address change together when the\n    56\t  canonical byte encoding lands. Unchanged, still open.\n    57\t\n    58\t### B. Materialization — `jing_test`, `mem_test`\n    59\t\n    60\t- B1 [D] `materialize!` derives the address itself, calls\n    61\t  `(put-content-fn address payload)`, and returns the address only after the\n    62\t  backend reports success.\n    63\t- B2 [D] A backend answers exactly `:inserted` or `:present`; any other\n    64\t  answer — `true`, `nil`, `:ok`, `\"inserted\"`, a map — is an invalid backend\n    65\t  result and throws.\n    66\t- B3 [D] On `:present` the stored value is read back and compared. An absent\n    67\t  read-back or an unequal value is an integrity failure that throws; nothing\n    68\t  is ever overwritten. Re-materializing an equal value is a no-op that\n    69\t  returns the same address.\n    70\t- B4 [D] What is stored is exactly the payload. No provenance, no source\n    71\t  identity, no stamp. Equal payloads arriving through different pool members\n    72\t  land on exactly one entry.\n    73\t- B5 [T→D] `nil` is a legal payload distinct from absence: `get` takes a\n    74\t  caller-supplied not-found and returns stored `nil` as `nil`.\n    75\t- B6 [T→D] A backend validates before it writes: the address must be a\n    76\t  segment address and must hash to the payload, else it throws and stores\n    77\t  nothing. This is the integrity precondition every backend, including a\n    78\t  future remote server, enforces at its own door.\n    79\t- B7 [T✗] `:dao.jing/content-missing` is a legal payload. This test exists\n    80\t  because an earlier sentinel was that keyword. The general rule — any value\n    81\t  is a payload, and the not-found sentinel is an opaque per-host object no\n    82\t  payload can equal — is B5; the specific keyword case is dropped.\n    83\t\n    84\t### C. Reads — `jing_test`\n    85\t\n    86\t- C1 [D] `get` accepts only segment addresses; arbitrary keys and mutable\n    87\t  roots throw **before the backend is consulted**.\n    88\t- C2 [D] `get` returns the caller-supplied not-found for an absent address\n    89\t  and only for that address.\n    90\t\n    91\t### D. Handle lifecycle — `jing_test`, `mem_test`, `file_test`\n    92\t\n    93\t- D1 [D] A handle is plain data: `:put-content-fn`, `:get-content-fn`, an\n    94\t  optional `:close-fn`. `materialize!` and `get` throw on a handle missing\n    95\t  the function they need.\n    96\t- D2 [D] `close!` delegates to `:close-fn` and returns `nil`; a handle without\n    97\t  one has nothing to release.\n    98\t- D3 [T→D] A backend's close is idempotent; after close, every entry point —\n    99\t  `materialize!`, `get`, and the raw fns — throws, and stored content is\n   100\t  neither cleared nor rewritten.\n   101\t- D4 [T✗] The handle carries a `:state` atom of shape `{:closed? :content}`,\n   102\t  and carries no `:stream` key. The observable is B4; the atom shape is\n   103\t  implementation. The rewrite exposes what B4 needs — a test-visible content\n   104\t  view for the mem backend, `records` (F5) for the file backend — and pins\n   105\t  neither the atom nor the absence of a key that guarded a design that no\n   106\t  longer exists.\n   107\t- D5 [T] Under JVM contention, N concurrent puts of one payload yield exactly\n   108\t  one `:inserted`, N−1 `:present`, and one stored entry — for both backends.\n   109\t\n   110\t### E. The intake pool and the observer — `dao/stream/observe_test` (core step), `jing_test` (pool), plus the pool drains in `mem_test`, `file_test`, `dht_test` and the five `dao.space` tests\n   111\t\n   112\tEach carries the side of the seam that guarantees it: **[step]** the core\n   113\t`dao.stream.observe/step`; **[pool]** `dao.jing`'s coordination over it;\n   114\t**[both]** guaranteed by the step and re-asserted by the pool.\n   115\t\n   116\t- E1 [D] [pool] The pool is supplied explicitly as `{:stream :cursor}`\n   117\t  members; the observer registers nothing, discovers nothing, and holds no\n   118\t  atom. Its state is plain data, `{:members [...] :next i}`, and\n   119\t  `(observer-state (:members st))` rebuilds it.\n   120\t- E2 [D] [both] The cursor is minted by the composition from an anchor of its\n   121\t  choosing and retained exactly as the stream returns it. The step guarantees\n   122\t  that a single observation advances only to the successor `next` returned,\n   123\t  or leaves the cursor untouched; the pool guarantees that `adopt-cursor` is\n   124\t  the only other way a member's cursor changes, and it takes a value the\n   125\t  stream handed out. No arithmetic, no inspection of shape, no fabrication,\n   126\t  on either side.\n   127\t- E3 [D] [pool] A member without a cursor, or whose stream lacks the reader\n   128\t  surface, is a composition defect: `observer-state` throws before any\n   129\t  operation.\n   130\t- E4 [D] [both] Outcomes are exactly `dao.stream/outcomes-next`. The step\n   131\t  classifies all seven as data; a read answer outside the set, or not an\n   132\t  outcome map, is classified as a `transport-error` defect with the raw\n   133\t  answer retained — the rule `forward-step`'s `malformed-result` already\n   134\t  applies — never thrown. The pool's signals are the same seven keywords.\n   135\t  Checked declaration-driven on both sides. (Revision 8 had the step throw\n   136\t  on an out-of-set outcome; `forward` had already got this right, and the\n   137\t  VM's throw-on-unexpected is its own policy above the seam.)\n   138\t- E5 [D] [step, structurally] `materialize!` runs before the cursor advances;\n   139\t  if it throws, the exception propagates and the caller's state is\n   140\t  untouched, so the same payload is reprocessed from the same cursor once\n   141\t  the backend succeeds. This is not a rule the pool remembers. The core step\n   142\t  runs the effect between the read and the advance and advances only on the\n   143\t  effect's success; a throwing effect propagates before any advance exists.\n   144\t  `forward-step` has had this shape since it was written — `recur\n   145\t  next-cursor` appears only in the write-`ok` branch (`forward.cljc:130`) —\n   146\t  and the VM has it from `run-on-stream`'s publish ordering\n   147\t  (`stream_observer.cljc:141-143`). One place, three callers.\n   148\t- E6 [D] [pool] Round-robin fairness: `observe-step!` starts at `:next`, walks\n   149\t  the pool once, processes at most one payload, and yields that member's\n   150\t  turn. A continuously ready member cannot starve another; blocked and ended\n   151\t  members never prevent later members from being checked.\n   152\t- E7 [T✗] Two equal-length ready members drain in strict `A B A B` order. One\n   153\t  schedule that satisfies E6; the design says scheduling never affects the\n   154\t  content set. The test may keep it as an illustration; it is not owed.\n   155\t- E8 [D] [pool] Empty pool → `blocked`. All members ended → `end`. A closed\n   156\t  member still yields its retained payloads, then `end`.\n   157\t- E9 [D] [both] `gap` and the three defect outcomes are reported immediately\n   158\t  with `:member i` — `gap` with its recovery cursor, the defects with the\n   159\t  raw outcome map under `:result` — leave the member's cursor unchanged,\n   160\t  move the scheduling index past the member, and are reported again on that\n   161\t  member's next turn. Nothing is auto-resynchronized. The step supplies the\n   162\t  raw read and the recovery cursor as data and moves nothing; the pool\n   163\t  decides to report and not adopt.\n   164\t- E10 [D] [both] The observer never interprets a payload. The step hands the\n   165\t  value to the effect untouched; the pool hashes and stores.\n   166\t- E11 [T] [composition] What the `dao.space` drains pin, and nothing more:\n   167\t  drain until `blocked` or `end`; a `gap` is fatal to that test composition.\n   168\t\n   169\t### F. The file backend — `file_test`\n   170\t\n   171\t- F1 [D] Each record is `[address payload]` appended to a file; a put is\n   172\t  acknowledged `:inserted` only after the write is flushed.\n   173\t- F2 [D] On open, the file is replayed into the content map. An equal\n   174\t  duplicate record is tolerated; an unequal record at an existing address\n   175\t  is a collision and the open fails.\n   176\t- F3 [D] An incomplete tail is truncated before replay — shorter than the\n   177\t  length prefix, a negative length, a length past end of file. After\n   178\t  truncation the surviving records replay and a subsequent put lands clean.\n   179\t  Correctness evidence for the truncation code; there is no stored content\n   180\t  to be compatible with, and the framing is free.\n   181\t- F4 [D] A complete record that cannot be decoded, is not a two-element\n   182\t  vector, carries a non-segment address, or does not hash to its payload\n   183\t  fails the open — closed, loudly.\n   184\t- F5 [T] Acknowledged inserts survive close and immediate reopen; a\n   185\t  `:present` put writes no record. The backend exposes `records`, the\n   186\t  decoded record vector of a path, as the test's view of the file.\n   187\t- F6 [T→D] D3 and D5 for this backend: idempotent close, throw after close,\n   188\t  serialized concurrent puts with exactly one record written.\n   189\t- F7 [T✗] The length-prefix layout, the `pr-str` encoding, the handle's `:log`\n   190\t  and `:write-lock` keys. Implementation, owed nowhere.\n   191\t\n   192\t### G. Deferred: the remote store\n   193\t\n   194\tInvariants for a remote content store are Decision 3's. None is built here.\n   195\t\n   196\t## Decisions\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '50,318p' test/dao/jing_test.cljc | nl -ba -v50\"","aggregated_output":"    50\t\n    51\t\n    52\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    53\t;; Content addressing\n    54\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    55\t\n    56\t(deftest sha256-known-answer\n    57\t  ;; Pins the actual digest function; load-bearing across hosts: :clj\n    58\t  ;; delegates to MessageDigest, :cljs to goog.crypt, :cljd to the\n    59\t  ;; hand-rolled\n    60\t  ;; SHA-256 in dao.jing. If that implementation drifts, cljd peers mint\n    61\t  ;; different segment-keys than JVM/JS peers for identical values and\n    62\t  ;; content addressing silently fractures. Vectors are the NIST/FIPS-180-4\n    63\t  ;; digests.\n    64\t  (testing \"published vectors\"\n    65\t    (doseq\n    66\t      [[in want]\n    67\t       {\"\" \"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855\",\n    68\t        \"abc\"\n    69\t        \"ba7816bf8f01cfea414140de5dae2223b00361a396177a9cb410ff61f20015ad\",\n    70\t        \"abcdbcdecdefdefgefghfghighijhijkijkljklmklmnlmnomnopnopq\"\n    71\t        \"248d6a61d20638b8e5c026930c3e6039a33ce45964ff2167f6ecedd419db06c1\",\n    72\t        \"hello world\"\n    73\t        \"b94d27b9934d3e08a52e52d7da7dabfac484efe37a5380ee9088f7ace2efcde9\"}]\n    74\t      (is (= want (jing/sha256 in)) (str \"sha256 of \" (pr-str in)))))\n    75\t  (testing \"a 65-byte input, which crosses the 64-byte block boundary\"\n    76\t    (is (= \"635361c48bb9eab14198e76ea8ab7f1a41685d6ad62aa9146d301d4f17eb0ae0\"\n    77\t           (jing/sha256 (apply str (repeat 65 \"a\")))))))\n    78\t\n    79\t\n    80\t(deftest segment-key-is-content-addressed\n    81\t  (testing \"the key is deterministic and order-insensitive\"\n    82\t    (is (= (jing/segment-key {:a 1, :b 2}) (jing/segment-key {:b 2, :a 1})))\n    83\t    (is (not= (jing/segment-key {:a 1}) (jing/segment-key {:a 2})))\n    84\t    (is (= \"segment\" (namespace (jing/segment-key {:a 1})))))\n    85\t  (testing \"and is total over non-map values, which are legal payloads\"\n    86\t    (doseq [v [42 \"hello\" :kw [1 2 3] nil #{:a}]]\n    87\t      (is (= (jing/segment-key v) (jing/segment-key v))\n    88\t          (str (pr-str v) \" must hash deterministically\"))\n    89\t      (is (= \"segment\" (namespace (jing/segment-key v)))))\n    90\t    (is (not= (jing/segment-key 42) (jing/segment-key \"42\"))\n    91\t        \"distinct values must not collide across types\")))\n    92\t\n    93\t\n    94\t(deftest segment-keys-are-readable-edn\n    95\t  ;; An EDN keyword name must not start with a digit, but a bare sha-256\n    96\t  ;; hex often does. A key that cannot survive print -> read poisons every\n    97\t  ;; EDN boundary content addresses cross.\n    98\t  (testing \"no minted key starts with a digit\"\n    99\t    (doseq [n (range 16)]\n   100\t      (let [k (jing/segment-key {:n n})]\n   101\t        (is (not (contains? (set \"0123456789\") (first (name k))))\n   102\t            (str k \" is not readable EDN\")))))\n   103\t  #?(:clj (testing \"a minted key survives print -> read\"\n   104\t            (let [k (jing/segment-key {:a 1})]\n   105\t              (is (= k (clojure.edn/read-string (pr-str k))))))))\n   106\t\n   107\t\n   108\t(deftest segment-hash-recovers-the-content-hash\n   109\t  (testing \"the hash is extractable from the key and matches the content\"\n   110\t    (let [v {:a 1}]\n   111\t      (is (= (jing/content-hash v) (jing/segment-hash (jing/segment-key v)))))))\n   112\t\n   113\t\n   114\t(deftest segment-hash-rejects-invalid-keys\n   115\t  (testing\n   116\t    \"segment-hash extracts the hash only from a valid\n   117\t            :segment/sha256-<64 lowercase hex> address; foreign namespaces,\n   118\t            malformed hashes, non-hex characters, and wrong lengths throw\n   119\t            instead of merely stripping a name prefix\"\n   120\t    (let [h64 (jing/segment-hash (jing/segment-key {:a 1}))]\n   121\t      (is (= 64 (count h64)))\n   122\t      (is (re-matches #\"[0-9a-f]{64}\" h64))\n   123\t      (doseq [bad [(keyword \"other\" (str \"sha256-\" h64))\n   124\t                   (keyword \"segment\" \"sha256-\")\n   125\t                   (keyword \"segment\" (str \"sha256-\" (subs h64 0 63)))\n   126\t                   (keyword \"segment\" (str \"sha256-\" h64 \"0\"))\n   127\t                   (keyword \"segment\" (str \"sha256-\" (str/upper-case h64)))\n   128\t                   (keyword \"segment\"\n   129\t                            (str \"sha256-\" (str/replace h64 (first h64) \\g)))\n   130\t                   :plain :segment/not-a-hash :segment/sha256-xyz 42 \"abc\" nil\n   131\t                   [1 2]]]\n   132\t        (is (thrown? #?(:clj Exception\n   133\t                        :cljs js/Error\n   134\t                        :cljd Object)\n   135\t              (jing/segment-hash bad))\n   136\t            (str \"segment-hash must reject \" (pr-str bad)))))))\n   137\t\n   138\t\n   139\t(deftest content-hash-is-order-insensitive\n   140\t  (testing \"equal maps print identically regardless of insertion order\"\n   141\t    (is (= (jing/content-hash {:a 1, :b 2}) (jing/content-hash {:b 2, :a 1})))\n   142\t    (is (= (jing/content-hash #{:x :y}) (jing/content-hash #{:y :x}))))\n   143\t  (testing \"distinct values differ\"\n   144\t    (is (not= (jing/content-hash {:a 1}) (jing/content-hash {:a 2}))))\n   145\t  (testing \"content-hash drives the segment key\"\n   146\t    (is (= (jing/segment-hash (jing/segment-key {:a 1}))\n   147\t           (jing/content-hash {:a 1})))))\n   148\t\n   149\t\n   150\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   151\t;; Handle API: materialize! / get / close!\n   152\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   153\t\n   154\t(deftest materialize-hashes-and-retrieves\n   155\t  (testing\n   156\t    \"the address is derived automatically from the payload and the\n   157\t            value round-trips unchanged\"\n   158\t    (let [h (mem-handle)\n   159\t          payload {:a 1, :b [1 2 3]}\n   160\t          address (jing/materialize! h payload)]\n   161\t      (is (= (jing/segment-key payload) address))\n   162\t      (is (= \"segment\" (namespace address)))\n   163\t      (is (str/starts-with? (name address) \"sha256-\"))\n   164\t      (is (= payload (jing/get h address ::missing))\n   165\t          \"the stored value is exactly the payload: nothing stamped in\"))))\n   166\t\n   167\t\n   168\t(deftest put-receives-the-derived-address-and-payload\n   169\t  (testing \"the backend effect is invoked as (put-content-fn address payload)\"\n   170\t    (let [seen (atom nil)\n   171\t          payload {:v 1}\n   172\t          h {:put-content-fn (fn [a p] (reset! seen [a p]) :inserted),\n   173\t             :get-content-fn (fn [_ _] nil)}]\n   174\t      (jing/materialize! h payload)\n   175\t      (is (= [(jing/segment-key payload) payload] @seen)))))\n   176\t\n   177\t\n   178\t(deftest materialize-is-idempotent\n   179\t  (testing\n   180\t    \"duplicate materialization returns the same address, reports\n   181\t            :present on the second write, and never overwrites\"\n   182\t    (let [results (atom [])\n   183\t          store (atom {})\n   184\t          payload {:x 42}\n   185\t          h {:put-content-fn\n   186\t             (fn [address p]\n   187\t               (let [r (if (contains? @store address) :present :inserted)]\n   188\t                 (swap! store assoc address p)\n   189\t                 (swap! results conj r)\n   190\t                 r)),\n   191\t             :get-content-fn (fn [address nf] (get @store address nf))}\n   192\t          a1 (jing/materialize! h payload)\n   193\t          a2 (jing/materialize! h payload)]\n   194\t      (is (= a1 a2))\n   195\t      (is (= payload (jing/get h a1 ::missing)))\n   196\t      (is (= [:inserted :present] @results)))))\n   197\t\n   198\t\n   199\t(deftest get-absent-returns-not-found\n   200\t  (testing\n   201\t    \"get returns the caller-supplied not-found for an absent content\n   202\t            address and only for that address\"\n   203\t    (let [h (mem-handle)\n   204\t          address (jing/segment-key {:never 1})]\n   205\t      (is (= ::missing (jing/get h address ::missing)))\n   206\t      (jing/materialize! h {:never 1})\n   207\t      (is (= {:never 1} (jing/get h address ::missing)))\n   208\t      (is (= ::missing (jing/get h (jing/segment-key {:other 2}) ::missing))\n   209\t          \"a present address leaves other addresses absent\"))))\n   210\t\n   211\t\n   212\t(deftest get-rejects-arbitrary-addresses\n   213\t  (testing\n   214\t    \"only :segment/sha256-... content addresses are valid reads;\n   215\t            arbitrary keys and mutable roots are outside DaoJing\"\n   216\t    (let [h (mem-handle)\n   217\t          payload {:x 1}\n   218\t          _ (jing/materialize! h payload)]\n   219\t      (is (= payload (jing/get h (jing/segment-key payload) ::missing)))\n   220\t      (doseq [bad [:root/pointer :plain :segment/not-a-hash :segment/sha256-xyz\n   221\t                   42 \"abc\" nil [1 2] {:k :v}]]\n   222\t        (is (thrown? #?(:clj Exception\n   223\t                        :cljs js/Error\n   224\t                        :cljd Object)\n   225\t              (jing/get h bad ::missing))\n   226\t            (str \"must reject \" (pr-str bad)))))))\n   227\t\n   228\t\n   229\t(deftest get-rejects-arbitrary-addresses-before-touching-the-backend\n   230\t  (testing \"validation happens before any backend call\"\n   231\t    (let [h {:put-content-fn (fn [_ _] :inserted),\n   232\t             :get-content-fn\n   233\t             (fn [_ _] (throw (ex-info \"backend must not be consulted\" {})))}]\n   234\t      (is (thrown? #?(:clj Exception\n   235\t                      :cljs js/Error\n   236\t                      :cljd Object)\n   237\t            (jing/get h :root/pointer ::missing))))))\n   238\t\n   239\t\n   240\t(deftest forced-collision-is-an-integrity-failure\n   241\t  (testing\n   242\t    \"a different value already seated at the address is never\n   243\t            overwritten and the mismatch is reported loudly\"\n   244\t    (let [address (jing/segment-key {:b 1})\n   245\t          h (mem-handle {address {:a 1}})]\n   246\t      (is (thrown? #?(:clj Exception\n   247\t                      :cljs js/Error\n   248\t                      :cljd Object)\n   249\t            (jing/materialize! h {:b 1})))\n   250\t      (is (= {:a 1} (jing/get h address ::missing))\n   251\t          \"the existing value is untouched\"))))\n   252\t\n   253\t\n   254\t(deftest present-without-readable-content-is-an-integrity-failure\n   255\t  (testing\n   256\t    \"a backend that reports :present but cannot read the value back\n   257\t            is inconsistent\"\n   258\t    (let [h {:put-content-fn (fn [_ _] :present),\n   259\t             :get-content-fn (fn [_ _] ::missing)}]\n   260\t      (is (thrown? #?(:clj Exception\n   261\t                      :cljs js/Error\n   262\t                      :cljd Object)\n   263\t            (jing/materialize! h {:x 1}))))))\n   264\t\n   265\t\n   266\t(deftest content-missing-keyword-is-a-legal-payload\n   267\t  (testing\n   268\t    \"the former sentinel keyword :dao.jing/content-missing is a legal\n   269\t            opaque payload: a backend that reports :present but then returns\n   270\t            not-found for the content address must throw, not fake success by\n   271\t            equating its not-found with the payload\"\n   272\t    (let [h {:put-content-fn (fn [_ _] :present),\n   273\t             :get-content-fn (fn [_ not-found] not-found)}]\n   274\t      (is (thrown? #?(:clj Exception\n   275\t                      :cljs js/Error\n   276\t                      :cljd Object)\n   277\t            (jing/materialize! h :dao.jing/content-missing)))))\n   278\t  (testing\n   279\t    \"a conforming backend stores the keyword as an ordinary payload,\n   280\t            idempotently\"\n   281\t    (let [h (mem-handle)]\n   282\t      (is (= (jing/segment-key :dao.jing/content-missing)\n   283\t             (jing/materialize! h :dao.jing/content-missing)))\n   284\t      (is (= (jing/segment-key :dao.jing/content-missing)\n   285\t             (jing/materialize! h :dao.jing/content-missing)))\n   286\t      (is (= :dao.jing/content-missing\n   287\t             (jing/get h\n   288\t                       (jing/segment-key :dao.jing/content-missing)\n   289\t                       ::missing))))))\n   290\t\n   291\t\n   292\t(deftest invalid-backend-result-is-rejected\n   293\t  (testing \"ambiguous truthiness is not a valid result vocabulary\"\n   294\t    (doseq [bad [true nil :ok \"inserted\" 1 {:status :ok}]]\n   295\t      (let [h {:put-content-fn (fn [_ _] bad), :get-content-fn (fn [_ _] nil)}]\n   296\t        (is (thrown? #?(:clj Exception\n   297\t                        :cljs js/Error\n   298\t                        :cljd Object)\n   299\t              (jing/materialize! h {:x 1}))\n   300\t            (str \"backend result \" (pr-str bad) \" must be rejected\"))))))\n   301\t\n   302\t\n   303\t(deftest close-delegates-and-is-optional\n   304\t  (testing\n   305\t    \"close! returns nil, delegates to :close-fn, and tolerates\n   306\t            repeated calls and handles without a close-fn\"\n   307\t    (let [closed (atom 0)\n   308\t          h (assoc (mem-handle) :close-fn (fn [] (swap! closed inc)))]\n   309\t      (is (nil? (jing/close! h)))\n   310\t      (is (nil? (jing/close! h)))\n   311\t      (is (= 2 @closed) \"close! delegates to :close-fn on every call\"))\n   312\t    (is (nil? (jing/close! (assoc (mem-handle) :close-fn nil)))\n   313\t        \"a handle without :close-fn has nothing to release\")\n   314\t    (is (nil? (jing/close! {:put-content-fn (fn [_ _] :inserted),\n   315\t                            :get-content-fn (fn [_ _] nil)})))))\n   316\t\n   317\t\n   318\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
